@@ -2520,7 +2520,7 @@ The main specification assumes that the composite type \(w=v+u\) is Gaussian. Th
 With observability held fixed at a positive level, a social multiplier different from one requires a type inference function that is not locally flat, \(\Delta'(w)\neq0\). More generally, when observability varies with distance, changes in either \(\mu(z,d)\) or \(\Delta(w^\ast)\) can generate a multiplier different from one. The purpose of the simulations below is to show that the multiplier is not specific to the Gaussian benchmark. It equals one only over regions where the induced type inference function is locally flat.
 
 \paragraph{Simulation design.}
-We compare two type distributions. The first is the Gaussian benchmark, \(w\sim N(0,1)\). The second is an equally weighted bimodal mixture of \(N(-\eta,1)\) and \(N(\eta,1)\), with \(\eta\) large enough that the two modes are distinct. For each distribution, we vary the net private benefit shifter \(b\) and compute (i) the equilibrium participation rate \(\bar y(b)\), (ii) the social image return \(\Delta(w^\ast(b))\), and (iii) the implied social multiplier \(M(b)\).
+We compare two type index distributions on the scale shown in Figure~\ref{fig:robustness-bimodal}. The first is a concentrated Gaussian benchmark, approximately \(w\sim N(0.4,0.2^2)\). The second is an asymmetric bimodal mixture, approximately \(0.25\,N(-2,0.2^2)+0.75\,N(0.4,0.2^2)\). For each distribution, we vary the net private benefit shifter \(b\) and compute (i) the equilibrium participation rate \(\bar y(b)\), (ii) the social image return \(\Delta(w^\ast(b))\), and (iii) the implied social multiplier \(M(b)\).
 
 \paragraph{Results.}
 Figure~\ref{fig:robustness-bimodal} shows the results. Under the bimodal distribution, there are intervals in which the cutoff moves through the low density gap between the two modes. In those intervals, the participation curve is locally flat, \(\Delta(w^\ast)\) is locally constant, and the social multiplier equals one. Outside these locally flat regions, the multiplier differs from one. Under the Gaussian benchmark, there is no such gap, and \(\Delta(w^\ast)\) varies smoothly with the cutoff.
